13:10:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s normal to worry about fitting in, but the “math people are all nerds” stereotype is much narrower than reality. Math departments tend to include all kinds of people—social, artistic, athletic, quirky, quiet, outgoing—who happen to enjoy solving hard problems. A few useful things for him to keep in mind: - **Choose the subject because he likes it, not because of an image.** Four years is a long time to study something just to avoid a stereotype. If math genuinely interests him, that matters more. - **Find his people, not one “type” of person.** He can join math club or study groups, but also keep up interests outside math: sports, music, volunteering, gaming, student organizations, etc. College makes it easier to meet people through many different communities. - **Math is more collaborative than people expect.** Talking through problems, working with classmates, and attending office hours can be a great way to make friends. - **He does not have to make math his entire personality.** Being a math major is just one part of who he is. - **Explore related fields too.** Math can connect to computer science, economics, physics, statistics, finance, data science, biology, cryptography, and more. A broad set of courses may help him see where he feels most at home. Ultimately, “fitting in” usually comes less from choosing the supposedly right major and more from finding friends and activities that suit him. If he enjoys math, he should give it a real chance rather than letting a stereotype decide for him.
